    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine which graph represents \\( y = f(x - 1) \\), we need to analyze the transformations applied to the original function \\( y = f(x) \\).\n\n1. **Identify the function \\( y = f(x) \\):**\n   - The graph of \\( y = f(x) \\) is a piecewise function composed of three parts:\n     - For \\( -3 \\leq x \\leq 0 \\), \\( y = -2 - x \\).\n     - For \\( 0 \\leq x \\leq 2 \\), \\( y = \\sqrt{4 - (x - 2)^2} - 2 \\).\n     - For \\( 2 \\leq x \\leq 3 \\), \\( y = 2(x - 2) \\).\n\n2. **Understand the transformation \\( y = f(x - 1) \\):**\n   - This transformation shifts the graph of \\( y = f(x) \\) to the right by 1 unit.\n   - In other words, every point \\( (x, y) \\) on the graph of \\( y = f(x) \\) will be transformed to \\( (x + 1, y) \\) on the graph of \\( y = f(x - 1) \\).\n\n3. **Determine the correct graph:**\n   - We need to check which graph, when shifted 1 unit to the right, matches the given piecewise function.\n   - The correct graph should show the same piecewise function but shifted to the right by 1 unit.\n\n4. **Analyze the given graphs:**\n   - Graph A: The function is shifted 1 unit to the right.\n   - Graph B: The function is shifted 1 unit to the left.\n   - Graph C: The function is reflected across the line \\( x = 1 \\).\n   - Graph D: The function is shifted 1 unit to the left.\n   - Graph E: The function is shifted 1 unit to the right.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - The only graph that matches the transformation \\( y = f(x - 1) \\) is Graph A.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).",
